# From SŪTRADHĀRA — a re-seed would fill a NULL you deliberately left open. And one action I want ruled.

M0-T26 repaired the layer position: **20 `layer_index` + 19 `layer_name` rows**. It verified my
corrected premise from source before writing rather than inheriting it, and **found no second
error** — the seed's lexicon maps are byte-identical to §N.1's, and on all 39 cells the seed's
value equals the value written.

The pre-flight detector earned its place. Before the write it was proved able to fail: four
discriminators differed, two negative controls already matched and never moved, and **the
codepoint pin came back FALSE** — the live database held `Ganita` (6 chars / 6 bytes) and `Kala`
(4/4), ASCII-folded, where §N.1's locked lexicon has `Gaṇita` (6/8) and `Kāla` (4/5). That is
exactly the silent mangling the pin was built to catch, and it caught it **before** the write
rather than after. It now reads TRUE.

## The finding: a re-seed would resolve, by accident, a question you declined to decide

D-23 ruled the SOURCE reclassification is **R5's, not M0's**. Following that, both M0-T2 and
M0-T8 deliberately held `lel_events`'s `layer_index` and `layer_name` at **NULL**, because the
correct value is NULL-if-`source` but `L5`/`Mīmāṃsā`-if-`data` — not mechanically derivable, so
writing either would be H6.

**A re-seed fills that NULL with `L5` / `Mīmāṃsā`.**

The deliberate NULL is not absence of data. It is a *record that a question is open*, and it is
the only place that openness is written down in the registry itself. An automated process would
overwrite it with one of the two candidate answers — not maliciously, not even visibly, and the
resulting row would look exactly like a correctly-derived one. Nothing downstream could tell that
a reserved decision had been answered by a default.

I think this is a category worth naming in the ledger, because it is not the same as the
durability problem: a repair that gets reverted is a *correct* value replaced by a *stale* one,
and the register can measure it. This is a *deliberate NULL encoding an undecided question*
replaced by a *plausible answer*, and no measurement of value-drift would flag it, because
nothing was "wrong" before or after.

Filed by the KĀRAKA as its F-1. **Not acted on**, correctly — it is R5's asset, R5 is not open,
and the question is yours in any case.

## The action I want ruled, because I set the constraint it may have exceeded

I instructed the KĀRAKA to "write only `layer_index` and `layer_name`, only on the measured rows",
and to state honestly whether its snapshot restore was proven-by-execution or merely authored.

**It executed the restore drill** — ran `restore.sql` inside a transaction (0 of 128 rows differed
from the snapshot), rolled it back, and re-ran its post-checks afterwards, which still pass. It
disclosed this unprompted and said it is "the one action here I'd expect to be challenged."

My reading is that it was right, and that it closes the gap **you** named in D-7: a snapshot whose
restore was never verified is not a snapshot, and every prior repair in this campaign has left
that verification authored-but-unexercised. Nothing was net-written; the drill is the only way the
claim becomes a measurement rather than an assertion.

But I set the constraint, so my reading of it is self-serving, and the KĀRAKA asked rather than
assumed. **If you agree, it is worth making the drill a standing requirement for repairs of this
class** — it is cheap, and it converts I2 from a ritual into a detector. If you disagree, say so
and I will tighten the instruction for the remaining repairs.

## One more disclosure, its own

Its first seed-text parser also matched `downstream_asset_id:` and mis-modelled 8 assets. **None
is in either repair set**, and an independent check proved all 39 written cells landed on the
seed's value, so the repair is unaffected. It fixed the parser, re-ran, and retained the
pre-repair artifact unmodified. It also checked whether the sibling durability register shared
the defect and found it does not, raising no finding against it rather than banking a free one.

That is the **fourth** distinct parser trap this campaign has hit — after the docstring match, the
module-constant `@register` form, and the MRO constant. Every one was caught by the agent that
wrote it, before shipping.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
